  &lt;body>
    &lt;p>
&lt;b>Definition of the tagged values for stereotypes used in the MVF profile with mappings to corresponding metamodel attributes&lt;/b>

    &lt;/p>

    &lt;p>
The types assigned to tagged value definitions on this diagram are UML primitives from CCM's standard &lt;a href="mdel://magicdraw_uml_standard_profile_v_0001" erit:display="CUSTOM_TEXT" erit:update="NO_UPDATE">UML profile,&lt;/a> as used by the corresponding &lt;a href="https://www.omg.org/spec/MVF/20220501/MVF.xmi">MVF XMI&lt;/a> attribute, unless changes are indicated by a pale blue note. These primitives are used in preference to RDF&amp;#160;based primitives (e.g. &lt;a href="mdel://_19_0_4_c480314_1686253385761_191483_6060" erit:display="CUSTOM_TEXT" erit:update="NO_UPDATE">rdf:langString&lt;/a>, &lt;a href="mdel://_19_0_4_c480314_1686253385872_932909_7023?projectName=MvfBeta1_0_ad-22-05-03&amp;elementName=Data+Type+Text&amp;metaType=DataType&amp;elementQName=Imported+Ontologies%3A%3ACommons+Text+Datatype+Ontology%3A%3AText">cmns-txt:Text&lt;/a>, which are based on &lt;a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/2004/REC-xmlschema-2-20041028/#string">xsd:String&lt;/a>; see &lt;a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/rdf11-concepts/#section-Datatypes">RDF 1.1&amp;#160;Recommendation, Section 5, Datatypes&lt;/a>). This choice is also guided by the decision to take the MVF&amp;#160;XMI&amp;#160;as the primary basis for this metamodel and helps&amp;#160;to ensure the portability of the UML&amp;#160;profile when deployed in environments without access to this metamodel. When generating RDF&amp;#160;based ontologies from UML&amp;#160;models built with this profile, consideration should be give to the choice of primitive that these attributes are mapped to - the equivalent RDF&amp;#160;properties are asserted indirectly via subsetting relations to attributes of classes in the MVF&amp;#160;XMI&amp;#160;metamodel, which in turn can be traced via further subsetting relations in the RDF&amp;#160;metamodel. Note that there is not a one to one correspondence between the attributes of the MVF&amp;#160;XMI&amp;#160;and &lt;a href="https://www.omg.org/spec/MVF/20220501/AboutMVF.rdf">MVF&amp;#160;RDF&lt;/a> classes (see &lt;a href="mdel://_19_0_4_c480314_1690542632684_541236_19547" erit:display="NAME" erit:update="AUTOMATIC_UPDATE">MVF Metamodel - RDF to XMI/PDF equivalent class mappings&lt;/a>). If in doubt, default to typing RDF&amp;#160;conversions with &lt;a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/2004/REC-xmlschema-2-20041028/#string">xsd:String&lt;/a>, but note this may result in information loss.

    &lt;/p>

    &lt;p>
&amp;#160;

    &lt;/p>

    &lt;p>
The tagged value to attribute mappings are split across several diagrams to improve readability. See also &lt;a href="mdel://_19_0_4_c480314_1691002418883_79386_12544" erit:display="NAME" erit:update="AUTOMATIC_UPDATE">MVF Base Profile - Tagged values mapped to metamodel attributes (vocabularyEntry)&lt;/a> and &lt;a href="mdel://_19_0_4_c480314_1693041418618_655612_11596" erit:display="NAME" erit:update="AUTOMATIC_UPDATE">MVF Base Profile - Tagged values mapped to metamodel attributes (mvfElement)&lt;/a>

    &lt;/p>

    &lt;p>
See &lt;a href="mdel://_19_0_4_c480314_1686178675778_956928_5355" erit:display="NAME" erit:update="AUTOMATIC_UPDATE">MVF Base Profile - Class stereotype hierarchy mapped to metamodel types (full)&lt;/a> for the inheritance structure of metaclasses and stereotypes.

    &lt;/p>

